WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.890
- Hi everyone.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:13.980
I'm Maurice Samuels, the
Director of the Yale Program

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.900
for the Study of Antisemitism,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.260
and on behalf of YPSA and the
Whitney Humanities Center,

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:20.950
it's my great pleasure to welcome you

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:24.190
to the first event of our
Benjamin and Barbara Zucker

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:27.440
webinar lecture series this
year, which will be devoted

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.420
to exploring the
relationship of antisemitism

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.940
to anti-Blackness and
other forms of prejudice.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.410
We have a superb lineup
of speakers planned,

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.670
who will guide us in a
conversation about the challenges

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.670
that Jews, Blacks, Asians, Muslims,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.870
and other minorities face in common,

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.540
and about the differences that divide us.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.100
Please make sure to sign
up for our mailing list

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.940
on the website for the Yale Program

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.690
for the Study of Antisemitism,

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:56.490
so you receive our webinar invitations

00:00:56.490 --> 00:01:00.450
and feel free to publicize
these far and wide.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.650
Just a few words before we get started

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.010
about how the event today will work.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.860
Our speakers will talk
for about 30 minutes,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.680
at which point they will
start taking questions

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.070
from the audience.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:16.140
Please submit your questions
anytime during the presentation

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.900
using the Q&amp;A function at
the bottom of your screen.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.670
They will only be visible to me

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.060
and I will submit them to speakers.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.730
The chat function is disabled.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:30.450
The audience will also remain
muted throughout the event

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.545
and we'll finish around 6:00 PM.

00:01:34.545 --> 00:01:39.545
So it's my great pleasure to
introduce today's speakers.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:45.140
First, Anthony Russell, here
he is, is a vocalist, composer

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.600
and arranger specializing in
music in the Yiddish language.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.440
This work has brought him to
Los Angeles, Philadelphia,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.830
Boston, Miami, New York,
Toronto, Montreal, London,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.690
Berlin, Copenhagen,
Warsaw, Krakow, Tel Aviv,

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.580
Symphony Space in New York City

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.530
and the Kennedy Center in Washington DC.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.630
Anthony's work with the Klezmer trio,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.280
Veretski Pass, resulted in "Convergence,"

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.550
an EP exploring a century
of African-American

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.110
and Ashkenazi Jewish music.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.630
Anthony lives in Massachusetts
with his husband,

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:19.993
Rabbi Michael Rothbaum.

00:02:20.841 --> 00:02:23.133
Next, we have Jeff Melnick.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.090
Here's Jeff.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:27.000
Jeff teaches American Studies

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.590
at the University of Massachusetts Boston,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.250
where he spends a fairly
large percentage of his time

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.170
these days on his work as a member

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.640
of the Faculty Staff
Union Executive Committee.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.800
In his scholarly life, he
has written a number of books

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.607
including two in the arena
of Black-Jewish relations,

00:02:44.607 --> 00:02:46.187
"A Right to Sing the Blues:

00:02:46.187 --> 00:02:50.600
"African-Americans, Jews
and American Popular Song,"

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.057
and "Black-Jewish Relations on Trial:

00:02:53.057 --> 00:02:56.860
"Leo Frank and Jim
Conley in the New South."

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.347
Most recently, he published

00:02:58.347 --> 00:03:00.960
"Creepy Crawling with the Manson Family,"

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.560
a cultural history of
the legacies of Manson

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.040
and his followers.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.660
At present, Jeff is
researching a book on race

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:10.650
and youth culture in Boston
in the years following

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.960
public school integration in the 1970s.

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.680
I'm extremely grateful
to both Anthony and Jeff

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.700
for helping us launch
our series and the title

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.280
of their discussion today

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.753
is "Shifting Affinities:
Cultural Investigation

00:03:24.753 --> 00:03:27.940
"of the Black-Jewish Relations Paradigm."

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.223
So I will now hand it
over to Jeff and Anthony.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.620
- First, I think I'd like to
establish my own observations

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:41.036
of certain underlying dynamics

00:03:41.036 --> 00:03:45.230
of the American Black-Jewish
relations paradigm,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:49.080
namely cultural rhetoric of
trans-historical connection

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.620
that imbues so many
discussions of the relationship

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.470
between Black and Jewish
populations in American space.

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:59.920
I feel like it's an underexamined
historical circumstance

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.240
that specifically, two disparate
diasporas happened to meet

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.440
on American soil, and of
course not just the diasporas,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.180
but the general rhetorical baggage,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.350
each of those diasporic identities entail

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.490
concerning loss of homeland, peoplehood,

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.020
prophetic narratives,
concepts of redemption

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.620
and freedom and the like.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.090
With specifically Biblical
cultural rhetoric,

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.040
as far as Black people are concerned,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.710
I've encountered certain
Jewish interlocutors

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.320
who call this phenomenon an appropriation,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.470
which I think is a misguided
term, in this case,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.490
as Jewish text and narrative tropes

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.200
were originally imposed
on enslaved Black people

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.360
under duress as a part
of the hegemonic dictates

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.370
of colonial Christianity.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:50.320
I do feel that it is very much in keeping

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.300
with the general ways of
being Black in the world,

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.690
that something that was
imposed on Black people

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:58.360
as an establishment of
order was summarily turned

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.330
into an article of
self-determination and freedom,

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:04.703
but that's a whole other
conversation for another day.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:10.150
Coming from the Jewish
side, in the Magid section

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.277
of the Passover Haggadah
is the following statement.

00:05:13.277 --> 00:05:16.077
"Whoever expounds on
the story of the Exodus

00:05:16.077 --> 00:05:19.180
"at length is worthy of praise."

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:22.640
So there is, of course
a Halakhic dimension

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:23.810
to this statement.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:26.800
The mitzvah is not merely
in knowing about the Exodus,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.480
but also in speaking about it,

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.927
and expounding upon that narrative.

00:05:30.927 --> 00:05:34.200
So I'd like to make the
contention that perhaps,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.230
when Jews encounter the phenomenon

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:39.350
of formerly enslaved people
in the United States,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:44.050
culturally, rhetorically
there was this quality

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:46.500
of Black people as living expositions

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.900
of the Exodus narrative.

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:53.070
Because of that, Black
people have become a site

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.410
of Jewish projection, identification,

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.390
and even, perhaps,
naturalization of historical

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:02.450
Jewish suffering on the American soil.

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.790
Rhetorically, Black people almost exist

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:09.300
as an atavistic enactor
of nascent peoplehood

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.000
after the ravages of systemic oppression,

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.460
a narrative most famously
sketched out, of course,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.130
in the Exodus narrative itself.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:22.130
I think this is partly why
facile and reductive comparisons

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.230
of the respective
socioeconomic trajectories

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:27.820
of Black and Jewish people are invoked,

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.310
oftentimes by Jewish critics,

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:34.310
because there's this
preexisting notion that somehow,

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.490
these two groups of people

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.500
are somehow intrinsically related,

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.200
even when their respective outcomes

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.950
are diametrically opposed.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:46.450
This is spelled out much
more explicitly, I think,

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:49.513
when we look at the history
of Black cultural expression

00:06:49.513 --> 00:06:53.240
and genre consumed and eventually,

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.366
produced as extensions
of Jewish expression

00:06:56.366 --> 00:07:00.230
as a sort of cultural rhetorical space

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:02.850
of establishing both
the Jewish connection,

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:07.850
and very importantly, Jewish
distance from Black people

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:11.997
in American social and cultural space.

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:14.200
And I'm very lucky to
be talking to Jeffrey

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.790
because this is something about which

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:17.963
he has written about quite ably.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:21.703
- Amazing.

00:07:21.703 --> 00:07:22.730
So I'll, I'll jump in there.

00:07:22.730 --> 00:07:25.870
And first of all, thank
Anthony for being the one

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.100
to kick this off.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.070
Thank everyone at Yale who organized this

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:32.900
and thank all of you who we can't see,

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.590
but are so grateful are joining
us for this conversation.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:40.710
So, you know, this is like
probably a kind of standard move

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:42.950
to make, but one of the
things I always try to do

00:07:42.950 --> 00:07:47.150
when I talk about Black-Jewish
relations is sort of start,

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:49.813
and Anthony's already
begun this conversation,

00:07:49.813 --> 00:07:52.930
open up this question
about what we mean when

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:54.260
we use that phrase.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:57.700
And something I tried to do
early in my career is replace

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.360
the phrase with a different phrase,

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.380
which, of course, couldn't possibly work.

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:04.110
The different phrase I wanted to introduce

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.260
was Black-Jewish relatedness,

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:07.690
as opposed to Black-Jewish relations,

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:09.800
because there's a conflation that happens,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.250
and Anthony, as I said,
Anthony has already begun

00:08:12.250 --> 00:08:13.790
to lay this out for you.

00:08:13.790 --> 00:08:15.176
There's a conflation that happens

00:08:15.176 --> 00:08:17.310
between two very different things.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.190
One is actual interaction,
is between Black people

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:21.450
and Jewish people.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.050
And then, of course, we
have to confront the fact

00:08:23.050 --> 00:08:25.053
that there are Black Jewish people.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.447
So there's the actual
meetings between Black people

00:08:28.447 --> 00:08:30.410
and Jewish people in the United States

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:31.610
in this overdetermined way

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:35.430
that Anthony has already
illumined for you.

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:39.360
And then there's the kind
of cultural imaginary,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.470
that all the work we do
to construct the idea

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.600
that somehow Black
people and Jewish people

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:48.500
share some really
consequential similarities.

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:50.210
Now let me just start with
Black-Jewish relations,

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.070
because that's a phrase that
I think most of the people

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:54.720
who are on this call
probably are familiar with.

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.010
You wouldn't have come
to a talk like this,

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.720
if you didn't know that
Black-Jewish relations is a thing,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:01.030
and had some weight in
American cultural history

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.730
and political history and
economic history, legal history.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.240
But it wasn't always a
thing with that name.

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:08.730
And so one of the things
I wanna do is sort of,

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:11.280
is just kind of unpack
that name a little bit,

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.530
Black-Jewish relations,
because my sense is

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:16.920
that we don't really have
that as a common phrase,

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.284
as a way of talking about
these actual interactions

00:09:20.284 --> 00:09:22.700
until the 1950s or 1960s.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.299
I mean, it gets going in the 1950s,

00:09:24.299 --> 00:09:26.080
but it really kicks off in the 1960s.

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:27.730
And one of the interesting things to note

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:30.510
is that they take off
because there's a sense

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.100
that develops, particularly amongst

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:34.260
certain well-placed Jewish people,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.600
that the relationship is falling apart.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.010
So there's a number of things
that happened in the 1960s.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:41.630
And I won't, you know, I don't
wanna create a whole archive

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.999
of this now in the limited
time we have together,

00:09:43.999 --> 00:09:46.620
but I'll say a few obvious things.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.320
There's a sort of clear recognition

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.480
that the kinds of alliances that brought

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.190
African-Americans and Jews
together in the world,

00:09:56.190 --> 00:10:00.700
of the popular culture, in
the world of jurisprudence,

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.210
legal work, those things weren't happening

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.600
with the depth and the
rapidity and the constancy

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.880
that they had happened in,
as had happened in the 1930s

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.440
and '40s, early 1950s.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.270
So there's this, there's a few crises

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.210
that happen in the 1960s.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:17.070
I'll just locate one for now.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.120
The Ocean Hill Brownsville
School Crisis of the 1960s,

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.150
where Black parents were
trying to gain control

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.860
of their local schools
and the teacher's union.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:26.502
And I can't believe I'm about
to say something critical

00:10:26.502 --> 00:10:29.640
of the teacher's union, now
that Mauri introduced me

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:32.288
as centering my own
teacher's union as crucial

00:10:32.288 --> 00:10:35.880
to my work in the world right now,

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:37.690
but the teacher's union, which was,

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:42.230
had a Jewish leader and a
very, a large population

00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:43.870
of Jewish teachers really resisted this,

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.352
because this felt like an incursion

00:10:45.352 --> 00:10:48.722
on their professional
control of the schools.

00:10:48.722 --> 00:10:50.650
And it got ugly in a
number of different ways,

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.210
and I don't want to go into
all the details of that,

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.570
but one of the things that
grew out of that event

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:56.950
and a number of other moments of that kind

00:10:56.950 --> 00:11:00.420
of direct conflict were
symposiums and lectures

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:04.310
and books and editorials,
and hand-wringing.

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.363
I mean, more than anything hand-wringing,

00:11:06.363 --> 00:11:11.310
and just about every
one of these symposiums,

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:13.520
symposium and lectures
was called something

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.560
like "What Went Wrong," right?

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.610
It always had that kind
of logic of inevitability

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:22.410
built into it, right,
that the relationship

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:25.980
between African-Americans
and Jews was somehow natural.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.960
So that brings me to the relatedness part,

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:30.920
because the naturalness
was not necessarily baked

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:33.030
into the actual interactions,

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:37.680
the naturalness was baked
into this sense of, and again,

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:39.960
I'm referring to Anthony's
initial commentary,

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:42.450
the naturalness is built
into this sense that somehow,

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:46.460
Blacks and Jews are deeply
similar to each other.

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:50.290
And it has its roots,
obviously in this 19th century,

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:53.164
African-American adoption of
certain sacred Jewish texts,

00:11:53.164 --> 00:11:57.570
but it also has 20th
century iterations, right?

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:00.548
It had the sense that
African-Americans and Jews

00:12:00.548 --> 00:12:04.910
are the most musical people
in the United States,

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:07.820
that African-Americans and
Jews are the most likely,

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:10.160
most likely groups to be
fighting for social justice

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.160
in the United States.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.670
So this thing that I'm
calling the cultural imaginary

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:16.300
developed, which tells us that more

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:18.520
than any other nonwhite groups,

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:21.280
and I'm using that word nonwhite

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:24.360
really advisedly now,
and in a contingent way,

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:26.587
and only really up until the early 1930s,

00:12:26.587 --> 00:12:29.860
the sense that African-Americans and Jews

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:31.710
are the two nonwhite
groups sort of most likely

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:33.950
to find each other and work together,

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.360
whether it's in the music industry

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:40.210
or the Civil Rights
Movement creates sort of,

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:44.780
on top of it, this super
structure of relatedness, right?

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:46.350
And so, I really tried to push this idea

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:48.220
that there's a relatedness
that's different

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.520
from the actual relations.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.750
Now, the problem, and I'm
gonna stop at it for now,

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:55.140
I'm gonna stop after I
say this one last thing

00:12:55.140 --> 00:12:57.470
is that it comes, there comes
a moment where the relations

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:59.970
break down enough that the relatedness

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:01.860
comes into question, as well.

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:06.250
And I'll timestamp that sometime
late 1960s, early 1970s.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:09.380
And we'll say that
there's this other thing

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:10.890
that happens that we'll
need to talk about,

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:14.444
which is that up until the
1950s, African-Americans and Jews

00:13:14.444 --> 00:13:17.148
tended to live really close to each other.

00:13:17.148 --> 00:13:18.500
I'm sitting here in Boston.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:21.108
So I'm thinking about Roxbury, Mattapan.

00:13:21.108 --> 00:13:24.100
I know a lot of you are
sitting in New Haven.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:25.990
So if you think about the closeness of,

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.428
let me see if I get this
right, I'm trying to remember,

00:13:28.428 --> 00:13:31.760
Westville to Newhallville,
neighborhoods in New Haven,

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:34.167
which are largely Jewish,
largely African-American.

00:13:34.167 --> 00:13:36.050
If you think about New York, you know,

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:38.440
thinking about Harlem and the succession

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:41.160
of African-Americans
coming up through Jews,

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:45.910
and there is this very
sort of every day contact

00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:47.330
that African-American and Jews have,

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:50.540
which kind of supports
this idea of relatedness,

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:53.211
but that really begins
to end in the 1950s,

00:13:53.211 --> 00:13:56.740
as Jews take part in what we
have to call White Flight.

00:13:56.740 --> 00:14:00.497
And Jews who are living in
urban areas like Roxbury

00:14:00.497 --> 00:14:04.190
and like Harlem and, you know,

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:05.070
sort of pick your neighborhood,

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:07.400
in the city that you're interested in,

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.670
they're not there anymore.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:11.440
They leave traces behind
because they still are involved

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.410
in businesses in those places,

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:16.040
and that makes things
even more complicated,

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:17.972
that the Jewish presence in
a lot of that neighborhood

00:14:17.972 --> 00:14:21.783
is now simply as
merchants or as landlords.

00:14:24.001 --> 00:14:26.560
And that makes it really
complicated to imagine

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.210
that Africans-Americans and
Jews are simply together,

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:31.620
the same, alike.

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:32.720
So I'm gonna step back
there and see if Anthony

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:33.963
wants to jump back in.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:40.873
Muted again.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:43.970
It looks like they're
unmuting us one at a time.

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:45.003
Yeah, yeah.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:46.853
- Got it.

00:14:46.853 --> 00:14:50.300
So this concept of an inherent relatedness

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:53.170
becomes endlessly
complicated and problematic

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:57.040
when one examines, how much
agency Black people have had

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:01.290
in this rhetorical relationship,
and how much material good

00:15:01.290 --> 00:15:03.680
this relationship has
actually rendered them.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:07.310
It becomes really problematic
when one considers

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:08.770
how these narratives of connection

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:12.490
so easily become a platform for concrete

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:14.840
and established recrimination.

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:16.440
Whenever Black people fell outside

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:19.070
of the invisible boundaries
of what is supposed to be

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:24.070
a familial connection, the pain
and the feelings of betrayal

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:28.627
that arise in the hearts
of Jewish observers

00:15:28.627 --> 00:15:31.050
of patterns of antisemitic thinking

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.950
in parts of the Black community,

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:36.610
I think these feelings
of betrayal are familial,

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:38.230
actually, in nature.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:43.230
How can you, a version of
myself, indulge in thinking

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:45.640
that potentially undermines yourself,

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.640
rather you as a version of myself?

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:56.160
And I think this unusual
kind of, I think you said

00:15:56.160 --> 00:16:01.130
a cultural imaginary sort
of relationship imbues a lot

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:06.130
of the rhetoric that the
monolithic Jewish community

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:10.660
has in relationship to the
monolithic Black community,

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.453
but not always vice versa.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:19.680
I'm willing to bet
Jeffrey is probably trying

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:21.770
to unmute himself at the moment.

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:23.920
- Yeah, and I don't, and
I don't wanna put anyone

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:25.574
on the spot here, but if you're saying,

00:16:25.574 --> 00:16:26.880
we're gonna try to actually
have a conversation,

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:29.620
so if there's any times
we can both be on the mic

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:31.860
at the same time, it looks
like maybe we are now.

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:33.852
That'd be great, thank you so much.

00:16:33.852 --> 00:16:38.500
So yeah, I wanna pick up on
the last thing Anthony said

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:42.130
and run with it a little bit,
this idea, and it brings us,

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.870
obviously, to the title of this seminar

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.490
and the purpose of our visit today,

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:50.146
this idea that there's
a very particular kind

00:16:50.146 --> 00:16:54.837
of Black antisemitism is a
question that I think Anthony

00:16:54.837 --> 00:16:57.080
and I both, you know, sort of wrestle with

00:16:57.080 --> 00:16:59.860
in our conversations and
our thinking about this.

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:02.070
And I think he said
something really important.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:05.250
I just wanna underscore it and
maybe extend it a little bit.

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:09.600
The idea that if there's
any Black disagreement

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:13.760
or Black distance from
a Jewish organization

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:16.885
or a Jewish movement
or a Jewish production

00:17:16.885 --> 00:17:19.340
that that's seen as a kind of betrayal

00:17:19.340 --> 00:17:21.573
and not just distance, disinterest.

00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:28.160
Agency is the word Anthony used,

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:29.730
and that seems perfect.

00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:33.300
And so there is this problem
with Black-Jewish relations,

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:35.987
as it's usually constituted, which is,

00:17:35.987 --> 00:17:37.570
and this is really tricky to talk about.

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:40.384
I wanna paraphrase something
Adolph Reed wrote in his book

00:17:40.384 --> 00:17:42.800
about Jesse Jackson,
because Jesse Jackson,

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:46.860
of course, he's one of those
kinds of African-Americans who,

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:50.440
when he did something that was construed,

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:53.470
and I don't necessarily want to relitigate

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:56.078
that whole case but like when
he when he said Hymietown,

00:17:56.078 --> 00:17:59.113
and there was this question
about whether Jesse Jackson

00:17:59.113 --> 00:18:02.554
was antisemitic, there was, this went on,

00:18:02.554 --> 00:18:04.740
this conversation went on for years,

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.780
and the question that some
Jews finally got to was,

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:09.790
does it really show, the
face that he even knows

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:13.600
the word Hymie, as a slur of Jews,

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:15.840
does it show that somehow,
he really is family,

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:17.910
that he is in close, right?

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:21.010
Because that, right, and so
one of the things Adolph Reed

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:23.704
underscored in his book
about Jesse Jackson,

00:18:23.704 --> 00:18:25.430
in one chapter where he was talking

00:18:25.430 --> 00:18:27.760
about the sort of Black
antisemitism question

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:29.750
is that one of the problems
with Black-Jewish relations

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:31.833
is that very often, it's a conversation

00:18:31.833 --> 00:18:34.593
that white Jewish people want to steer

00:18:34.593 --> 00:18:37.030
from both sides, right?

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:38.300
They wanna say their piece,

00:18:38.300 --> 00:18:40.590
but they also want someone
who will wanna ventriloquize

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:43.300
what African-Americans should
be saying and doing, right?

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:45.120
There's the agency question again.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:46.833
And so I'm really, it's really
uncomfortable to talk about

00:18:46.833 --> 00:18:50.927
because it does play into some,
it does play into some bad,

00:18:50.927 --> 00:18:53.436
the analysis of this plays into some bad

00:18:53.436 --> 00:18:56.420
old antisemitic images of Jews as,

00:18:56.420 --> 00:18:58.140
well Jews as puppetmasters, you know,

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:01.090
sort of controlling the
discourse, the Svengali,

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:03.920
you know, kind of construct

00:19:03.920 --> 00:19:05.820
but it's a really important
question to ask, right?

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.937
Because one of the things
that, in Black-Jewish relations

00:19:07.937 --> 00:19:10.440
we always talk about
is that Jews were there

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:11.360
for African-Americans,

00:19:11.360 --> 00:19:13.670
they were there at the
founding of the NAACP.

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:15.370
They were there on the legal team,

00:19:16.261 --> 00:19:18.750
in the Brown vs. Board of Ed discussion.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:21.503
They were there, obviously in, in the,

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:27.620
enrolling voters drive in
Mississippi in 1964, right?

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:30.946
These are sacred texts of
Black-Jewish relations,

00:19:30.946 --> 00:19:34.150
and one of the problems
with the way we talk

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:38.020
about that whole history is
that it becomes impossible

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:40.400
to figure out where sort
of the Jewish contribution

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:43.223
begins and ends, because
it becomes a sort of like,

00:19:44.574 --> 00:19:46.700
it becomes the water, yeah.

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:47.910
- It's a communal property.

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:49.860
It becomes a communal property.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:51.314
- Perfect, yeah, yeah.

00:19:51.314 --> 00:19:52.650
- And it's...

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:55.120
It's a problematic communal property,

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.470
because what it does is
it conflates the actions

00:19:57.470 --> 00:20:02.470
of a statistically small amount
of very grave individuals

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:07.830
who decided to actually
move against the tide

00:20:07.830 --> 00:20:12.470
of where American, the
American Jewish community

00:20:12.470 --> 00:20:17.380
was socially, and to show
up on behalf of Black people

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:21.290
for the purpose of racial equality,

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:24.330
and what has happened, of
course, in the intervening years

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:27.650
is that their actions have been conflated

00:20:27.650 --> 00:20:31.930
with the entire American Jewish community.

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:36.100
And what that has created is a rhetoric

00:20:36.100 --> 00:20:41.100
of indebtedness that Black people have

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.035
in relationship to the Jewish community.

00:20:44.035 --> 00:20:47.760
And I think it's really
important for me to say.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:52.410
and for people to hear that
this is an unfortunate platform

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:55.380
on which to build any sort of solidarity

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:59.040
around the issues of antisemitism

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:02.073
and acting on anti-Blackness.

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:05.263
- Right.
- Black people

00:21:05.263 --> 00:21:07.640
in the United States already
have and continue to have

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:09.950
a very unfortunate experience

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:12.904
with rhetorics of indebtedness.

00:21:12.904 --> 00:21:15.710
You'll see this crop up
in any number of spaces

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:19.100
where people have decided that actually,

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:23.100
it was a good thing that
entire populations of Africans

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:24.480
were brought to the United States,

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.500
because pick your period.

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:30.539
They found Jesus, that
would be a very 19th century

00:21:30.539 --> 00:21:32.240
sort of version of that idea.

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:36.920
Maybe later, they became
acquainted with Western society

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:38.773
and culture to their benefit.

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:41.470
You can go on Twitter right now,

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:42.960
and find somebody who's probably saying

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.750
something to this degree.

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:45.583
If you hadn't come to the United States,

00:21:45.583 --> 00:21:49.130
you would be in Ethiopia
and you'd be starving,

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:51.650
or you would be a child soldier.

00:21:51.650 --> 00:21:56.650
Given that so much of the
rhetoric around Black people

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:02.303
in the United States has come
from a place of indebtedness,

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:06.350
a rhetorics, and a
politics of indebtedness

00:22:06.350 --> 00:22:09.564
in relationship to a
response to antisemitism

00:22:09.564 --> 00:22:14.400
is undermining and is not sustainable

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.840
and does not build the kind of solidarity

00:22:17.840 --> 00:22:22.196
that is needed to arrest
patterns of antisemitic thinking

00:22:22.196 --> 00:22:26.850
in the Black community, if I
could talk about a community

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:30.200
in a complete monolith, and
the way that one almost has to

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:32.750
in these kinds of conversations

00:22:32.750 --> 00:22:33.763
- Right. That's brilliant, yeah.

00:22:33.763 --> 00:22:36.490
I mean, just the very familiarity we have

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:38.720
with the phrase Black antisemitism,

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:41.597
you know, James Baldwin
decades ago, 50 years ago,

00:22:41.597 --> 00:22:44.250
was trying to remind us that you know,

00:22:44.250 --> 00:22:46.190
if Black people are antisemitic,

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:47.623
it's because they're
anti-white and that's something

00:22:47.623 --> 00:22:49.750
just like a lot of Jews
don't like to hear, right?

00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:53.040
'Cause it upsets a
certain Jewish narrative

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:57.350
of marginalization, of
kind of special kind

00:22:57.350 --> 00:23:00.480
of community place
that's not purely white,

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.140
but that indebtedness is a real problem

00:23:02.140 --> 00:23:04.350
because it plunges us, all of us,

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:06.520
into these situations where Black people,

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:09.840
in this very ritual, narrow,
ritualized narrow way,

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:14.290
where Black people are sort
of urged, you know, pushed,

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:17.660
encouraged, pick your verb, to apologize

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:20.660
for other Black people,
for bad Black people.

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:23.300
And that's a really bad ritual
that I think we really need

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:25.610
to sort of think harder about, right?

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:28.820
And this comes up, you know,
I'm old enough that I feel

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:31.930
like I can sort of track
cycles of every five years,

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:35.520
some mainstream Black leader
has to disavow Louis Farrakhan.

00:23:35.520 --> 00:23:37.370
And it's like, you know, that's something

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:38.410
we need to get past, right?

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:40.420
'Cause Louis Farrakhan
is really important,

00:23:40.420 --> 00:23:42.870
has a lot of followers, but he just,

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:44.980
he's not at the heart of
mainstream Black experience

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:46.030
in the United States.

00:23:46.910 --> 00:23:49.160
- Good luck trying to
make that case, though.

00:23:51.012 --> 00:23:54.580
I think there are people in
the American Jewish community

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:56.650
who think we have pictures
of Farrakhan on the wall.

00:23:56.650 --> 00:24:00.973
Like he's the Lubavitcher
Rebbe, and it's not like that.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:07.430
I think there are spaces
in the Black community

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:10.300
in which his leadership is
looked on with a certain amount

00:24:10.300 --> 00:24:12.173
of dubiousness and derision.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:19.003
It's, I have a lot of
problems with kind of,

00:24:20.530 --> 00:24:21.760
in the formation and mobilization

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:24.360
of a monolithic concept
like Black antisemitism,

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:26.150
because I don't think it's helpful

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:31.150
or particularly effective
against fighting patterns

00:24:31.840 --> 00:24:34.640
of antisemitic thinking in
parts of the Black community.

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:39.170
Establishing a phenomenon of
antisemitism in ethnic terms

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:42.070
gives it the dimensions of
something that is intrinsic

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:47.070
to that ethnicity, as if
in each Black person lurks

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:51.709
an innate antisemite, who
must be tamed, exorcized

00:24:51.709 --> 00:24:52.593
and denounced.
- Right.

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:57.650
- The concept of Black antisemitism

00:24:57.650 --> 00:25:01.450
makes antisemitism not into an idea

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:05.000
or a prominent person or
persons who is disseminating

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.010
these ideas among the Black community,

00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:09.660
but rather it turns patterns
of antisemitic thinking

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:12.200
into an amorphous phenomenon, which,

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:14.350
while rhetorically useful,

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.170
actually undermines the
potential to address

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:19.033
and dismantle patterns
of antisemitic thinking.

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:25.710
Black antisemitism is much
more useful as a phrase

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:29.050
and as a rhetorical
device than it is as a way

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.360
of actually fighting patterns
of antisemitic thinking.

00:25:32.360 --> 00:25:33.193
- That's right, that's right.

00:25:33.193 --> 00:25:35.380
And I mean, just for our purposes today,

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:37.290
to try to start focusing on this thing

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:38.750
we call Black-Jewish relations,

00:25:38.750 --> 00:25:40.730
I was thinking back to moments in my own

00:25:40.730 --> 00:25:43.740
sort of scholarly life when
when there were these eruptions

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:50.640
of mourning of worry about
Black-Jewish relations,

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:53.510
about Black antisemitism
and one of the major ones

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:55.320
that I'm sure many people
on this call remember

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:58.520
were the Crown Heights
Riots of 1991, which again,

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:00.750
without getting into
the granular details of,

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:02.723
brought into conflict Hasids

00:26:04.470 --> 00:26:06.640
and a Black community in Brooklyn

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:09.220
that was largely West Indian, right?

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:11.160
And that came to stand for,

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:15.040
in this synecdochical way,
I forgot that word, right

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:17.100
for all of Black-Jewish relations,

00:26:17.100 --> 00:26:21.740
two very marginal communities
for each group, right?

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:24.180
West Indian, mostly immigrant population,

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:26.725
who came to stand for
Black people in the U.S.

00:26:26.725 --> 00:26:28.260
And Hasids, who came to stand for Jews.

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:30.240
Hasids have not been in the
Black-Jewish relations game,

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:31.980
you know, that we've been outlining

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:33.630
for this history, right?

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:35.980
Like they just get pulled
on into that narrative

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:37.300
in that moment because
they're sort of visible,

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:40.520
obviously visibly Jewish,
and that's something,

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:41.890
you know, as a historian,
I always wanna try

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:43.240
to bring to the table and say like,

00:26:43.240 --> 00:26:44.950
when we're talking about
Black-Jewish relations,

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:46.550
at least let us try to specify.

00:26:46.550 --> 00:26:48.060
And Anthony was trying to do that before,

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:49.380
when he was sort of reminding us

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:52.080
that it's a very small percentage,

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:55.600
a particular kind of politically
engaged Jewish people

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:57.890
who have been involved
in that main, you know,

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:00.680
sort of historical activity
of civil rights work.

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:04.580
It's a very small group of Jewish people

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:07.057
who were involved with the certainly,

00:27:07.057 --> 00:27:11.060
you know, the key legal cases
of the late '40s, early '50s.

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:12.540
So that's the thing I
want to remind us of,

00:27:12.540 --> 00:27:15.240
is to sort of think about
who we're trying to catch

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:17.530
in this net when we cast this net

00:27:17.530 --> 00:27:19.110
called Black-Jewish relations

00:27:20.938 --> 00:27:25.523
- I'd like to bring up
the fact that usually,

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:30.020
in evocations of the Golden
Age of Black-Jewish relations,

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:32.470
of course, Rabbi Abraham Joshua Heschel

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:36.890
always makes a starring appearance.

00:27:36.890 --> 00:27:40.230
I've had the virtue and the pleasure

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:44.270
of knowing his daughter, Susanna Heschel,

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:47.960
who is an amazing scholar
and over Shabbos dinner,

00:27:47.960 --> 00:27:50.110
we were talking about this very subject.

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:55.110
And she said very directly,
that if you aren't doing

00:27:55.580 --> 00:28:00.580
the work of creating racial
justice in this country,

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:03.473
you don't get to name check her father,

00:28:05.530 --> 00:28:10.530
which means that a lot of
rhetoric that has been created

00:28:12.023 --> 00:28:16.090
around this issue would
instantaneously disappear,

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:21.090
because oftentimes, this
affinity that these two groups

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:23.660
have for each other is
often expressed in terms

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:27.410
that are either approaching

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:31.120
or have surpassed the half century mark.

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:34.580
What do you do with an affinity

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:37.703
when it's about two generations back?

00:28:38.856 --> 00:28:39.720
- Right.

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.010
That's a great question.

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:42.460
That's a great question.

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:45.983
I just wanna note, and I don't
know if it's time or not,

00:28:45.983 --> 00:28:47.600
that we're a little past 5:30,

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:49.071
and I don't know if
there's questions coming up

00:28:49.071 --> 00:28:51.200
in the chat yet, 'cause it's hard for me

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:52.500
to see where they are.

00:28:52.500 --> 00:28:56.639
And I know that Anthony
and I can keep going until

00:28:56.639 --> 00:29:00.010
somebody says end meeting,
but I also wanna make sure

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:01.980
that we're going to leave
enough time for other people

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:03.231
to get into the conversation.

00:29:03.231 --> 00:29:05.620
- [Mauri] You guys can,
this is Mauri talking,

00:29:05.620 --> 00:29:07.210
you guys can keep going
for a little while longer

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:08.043
and then I'll jump in.

00:29:08.043 --> 00:29:09.480
- Okay, you'll jump in, okay, great.

00:29:09.480 --> 00:29:11.070
Okay, just wanted to check on that.

00:29:11.070 --> 00:29:14.730
So, you know, one of the things I'm gonna,

00:29:14.730 --> 00:29:16.870
I want to put another
term into the conversation

00:29:16.870 --> 00:29:18.950
that I feel like we've
been dancing around,

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:21.081
but haven't quite said, which is class,

00:29:21.081 --> 00:29:24.370
and we're not gonna be able to have

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:26.770
a forthright conversation
about this thing called,

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:29.370
so-called Black antisemitism
or Black-Jewish relations

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:30.560
more generally without talking

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:33.534
about the very different class
trajectories of white Jews

00:29:33.534 --> 00:29:36.034
and African-Americans in
the United States, right?

00:29:36.034 --> 00:29:40.020
Maybe it goes without saying,
but it's one of things

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:41.490
I was trying to index
when I talked a little bit

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:44.640
about white flight, which
is this, this period

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:49.170
and this formation that we
try to capture with this name,

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:52.240
Black-Jewish relations is
really rooted in this time

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:55.108
when Americans Jews were city people,

00:29:55.108 --> 00:29:59.840
and were sort of working
class to lower middle class.

00:29:59.840 --> 00:30:02.463
And we're in this moment now
where the class differential

00:30:02.463 --> 00:30:05.580
between white Jews and African-Americans

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:09.382
in the United States is so stark
that it's almost impossible

00:30:09.382 --> 00:30:12.642
to sort of have the conversation
without at least noting

00:30:12.642 --> 00:30:16.400
that the two communities, it's
more than two communities,

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:19.320
obviously, but for the shorthand,
two communities are living

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:22.065
literally living in very different places.

00:30:22.065 --> 00:30:24.850
They're occupying very
different vocations.

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:26.990
I know I'm painting with a very broad,

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:28.768
in very broad strokes right now,

00:30:28.768 --> 00:30:31.490
but, but there is, and I feel like Anthony

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:33.530
was sort of outlining
this a little bit before,

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:36.680
this idea that Blacks and
Jews aren't so different

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:38.170
from each other, and not only
are they not so different,

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:41.440
but there's this kind of
like amazing cultural energy

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:46.380
that convinces us that
they're almost the same,

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:49.070
and that's really dangerous, to my mind.

00:30:49.070 --> 00:30:52.060
It makes it impossible
for African-Americans

00:30:52.060 --> 00:30:54.489
to kind of find good
spaces in U.S. culture,

00:30:54.489 --> 00:30:57.560
as speaking subjects,
telling their own stories,

00:30:57.560 --> 00:30:59.262
and not telling a story
that that's kind of like,

00:30:59.262 --> 00:31:01.517
that we always, that we know already

00:31:01.517 --> 00:31:03.183
that's already been scripted.

00:31:04.640 --> 00:31:08.730
- I think it's very easy
to kind of want to...

00:31:10.170 --> 00:31:12.340
It's very easy for people
to want to have a discussion

00:31:12.340 --> 00:31:13.963
about Jews in the United States

00:31:13.963 --> 00:31:16.870
and the relationship to assimilation

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:20.750
into greater American culture,

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:25.230
but I think what is not
often discussed is that,

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:26.420
in order to do that,

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:29.370
there was a preexisting racial hierarchy

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:33.610
that either had to be
passively or actively accepted

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:39.840
as almost the fare for kind of arriving

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:45.100
into full American assimilation,

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:48.480
and a lack of engagement with that,

00:31:48.480 --> 00:31:53.480
I think, as a community, or
really, with that as an idea,

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:56.830
once again, undermines the ability

00:31:56.830 --> 00:31:59.730
for these two communities, so I can speak

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:02.320
about these two people and in a monolith

00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:04.260
like we have to throughout
this conversation,

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:06.490
it undermines the ability for us to have

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:10.270
a historically honest conversation
about where we have been

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:12.922
and where we could possibly go as far

00:32:12.922 --> 00:32:17.922
as joining each other
in the work of creating

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:20.400
racial equality in this country.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:21.233
- Right.

00:32:21.233 --> 00:32:22.800
- Okay, I'm gonna jump in here,

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:26.280
'cause we do have a bunch
of questions from people.

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:28.280
And I would just ask, I
noticed that some people

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:31.980
have raised their hand, but
since I can't call on people,

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.804
if you could type your
questions in the Q&amp;A,

00:32:34.804 --> 00:32:37.906
so if you see that little
Q&amp;A button at the bottom,

00:32:37.906 --> 00:32:42.070
you can type questions there,
and I will ask the panelists.

00:32:42.070 --> 00:32:43.370
So we have a bunch of questions.

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:46.130
This is really such a
fascinating conversation,

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:49.590
and I think we're all just
so grateful to both of you

00:32:49.590 --> 00:32:53.270
for your thoughtfulness
about these issues.

00:32:53.270 --> 00:32:57.560
So one question that I
had, and that I see also

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:01.700
that some of the people
in the audience have had

00:33:01.700 --> 00:33:04.780
is I think people were a
little bit puzzled by something

00:33:04.780 --> 00:33:08.650
you said, Jeff, about how
Jews weren't really white

00:33:08.650 --> 00:33:12.080
until the 1930s, or maybe
weren't seen as white.

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:14.760
And we're wondering if you
could just say something

00:33:14.760 --> 00:33:17.720
about how Jews became white.

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:22.720
Did Black people ever
not see Jews as white?

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:24.160
Did...

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:26.810
You know, how do we parse

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:29.690
this idea of Jews as almost white

00:33:29.690 --> 00:33:32.230
but not quite white
enough for white America?

00:33:32.230 --> 00:33:34.073
- Mm-hmm, great, yeah, that's,

00:33:35.010 --> 00:33:36.730
thank you anybody who's
thinking about that

00:33:36.730 --> 00:33:37.620
and asking the question.

00:33:37.620 --> 00:33:38.997
Thank you, Mauri, for
putting it on the table.

00:33:38.997 --> 00:33:43.110
You know, Jewish whiteness
is a really complex,

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:44.535
historical question.

00:33:44.535 --> 00:33:46.750
I've written about it, I see
at least two other people

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:48.700
on the call who have written about it,

00:33:48.700 --> 00:33:51.840
as I scroll down, and I'm
sure, probably more some folks

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:53.690
who maybe, I don't know.

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:55.012
So this is something that historians

00:33:55.012 --> 00:33:58.540
really got interested in, 1980s, 1990s,

00:33:58.540 --> 00:33:59.910
definitely earlier, as well.

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:02.050
And, there's this sort of
clear sense that at least

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:04.740
up until the 1920s, Jews occupied
some kind of middle space.

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:09.233
Jews are not the only not sort
of distinct group in the U.S.

00:34:09.233 --> 00:34:12.530
We've got great historians
of Italian-American life,

00:34:12.530 --> 00:34:16.910
great historians of, you
know, Armenian-American life

00:34:16.910 --> 00:34:18.260
who have tried to parse this question.

00:34:18.260 --> 00:34:21.093
Now, what do we do about
these groups that immigrated

00:34:21.093 --> 00:34:25.240
as part of the Great Wave
starting in the 1880s,

00:34:25.240 --> 00:34:27.643
so what we'd generally
call the New Immigration,

00:34:27.643 --> 00:34:29.410
and were not Anglo-American?

00:34:29.410 --> 00:34:30.470
And Jews were clearly part of that,

00:34:30.470 --> 00:34:32.612
Eastern European Jews, in particular,

00:34:32.612 --> 00:34:34.320
the German Jews who came earlier,

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:35.550
a little bit of a different story

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:36.830
but Eastern European Jews were seen,

00:34:36.830 --> 00:34:38.830
were definitely seen as
a distinct population.

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:40.856
Up through the '20s, there
was plenty of people,

00:34:40.856 --> 00:34:44.220
anthropologists, politicians, journalists,

00:34:44.220 --> 00:34:46.690
who were still referring
to Jews as a race, right?

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:49.390
That's obviously like not an
understanding that we have now

00:34:49.390 --> 00:34:52.630
of Jews, and the process
by which Jews stopped

00:34:52.630 --> 00:34:54.540
being publicly recognized as a race,

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:55.790
it's obviously not overnight.

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:57.370
We can't put a year on it.

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:59.480
We can, you know, make a,

00:34:59.480 --> 00:35:01.220
we can put some frames around it, right?

00:35:01.220 --> 00:35:05.160
We can certainly say
that by the 1930s, 1940s,

00:35:05.160 --> 00:35:07.410
as Jews are sort of moving
up the economic ladder,

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.610
certainly as news of
the genocide in Europe

00:35:10.610 --> 00:35:14.150
starts making its way to the
United States, in the 1950s,

00:35:14.150 --> 00:35:16.620
it kind of helps frame
this idea that Jews need

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:18.940
to be sort of like, we need to underscore

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:20.650
the safety of Jews in the United States.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:22.479
And part of the safety
is understanding Jews

00:35:22.479 --> 00:35:25.160
as white people, and then really, I think,

00:35:25.160 --> 00:35:27.458
with the suburbanization of the 1950s,

00:35:27.458 --> 00:35:29.380
it becomes impossible to think of Jews

00:35:29.380 --> 00:35:31.336
as anything other than, just another,

00:35:31.336 --> 00:35:33.280
what, in the 1960s and '70s,

00:35:33.280 --> 00:35:36.060
we start more frequently referring

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:37.930
to as an ethnic group, right?

00:35:37.930 --> 00:35:40.970
And so Jews are like
Italians, or like Irish

00:35:40.970 --> 00:35:44.852
or like various other hyphenated groups

00:35:44.852 --> 00:35:46.490
that are clearly white.

00:35:46.490 --> 00:35:48.540
You know, this holds for
immigrant groups up until,

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:51.490
basically 1965, when the
immigration law changes.

00:35:51.490 --> 00:35:54.071
And there's a huge wave of
people from Latin America,

00:35:54.071 --> 00:35:56.810
Asia, the Caribbean, Africa, and so on,

00:35:56.810 --> 00:35:59.660
who are hyphenated Americans
and clearly not white

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:01.290
in American terms.

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:02.123
So that's a quick answer.

00:36:02.123 --> 00:36:03.280
I could go on, but yeah.

00:36:04.440 --> 00:36:05.500
- No, that's great.

00:36:05.500 --> 00:36:06.333
That's...

00:36:07.646 --> 00:36:10.657
Another question from someone is how,

00:36:11.660 --> 00:36:15.410
someone who grew up in
the South and is you know,

00:36:15.410 --> 00:36:19.460
wondering about whether we
can say the Jews in the South

00:36:19.460 --> 00:36:24.460
adopted the prejudice of
white non-Jewish southerners.

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:26.613
And so I think we can say, you know,

00:36:26.613 --> 00:36:28.670
where most Jews came from,

00:36:28.670 --> 00:36:30.540
they probably didn't have many opinions

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:31.690
of Black people at all.

00:36:31.690 --> 00:36:34.740
And so how did, can we
trace that kind of history

00:36:34.740 --> 00:36:38.463
of anti-Black prejudice among Jews?

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:41.170
How would we tell that story?

00:36:41.170 --> 00:36:44.850
- In the historical record,
you find many proofs

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:48.680
of a general amount of bafflement

00:36:48.680 --> 00:36:53.510
from the Southern Jewish
community in the face

00:36:53.510 --> 00:36:56.830
of all of these Northerners
who came over the course

00:36:56.830 --> 00:37:01.830
of Mississippi Summer,
because to a certain extent,

00:37:02.020 --> 00:37:04.653
they had already figured out,

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:09.690
in decades and centuries previous,

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:14.690
what their specific place
was in the racial hierarchy

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:16.890
of the American South.

00:37:16.890 --> 00:37:21.890
And they knew that that
level of interaction

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:26.090
with Black people put that in peril.

00:37:26.090 --> 00:37:29.580
So there is that, I mean, that is a part

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:31.800
of the historical record
is that all of these,

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:34.650
you know, Jews from the North
came and the Jews in the South

00:37:34.650 --> 00:37:35.850
were like, who are these people?

00:37:35.850 --> 00:37:38.201
What are they doing? What is this about?

00:37:38.201 --> 00:37:39.876
- [Jeff] Right, right.

00:37:39.876 --> 00:37:41.430
- And I should just say actually,

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.520
one of the people in the
audience pointed out that,

00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:47.270
of course you know, white people,

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:51.330
or Jews have had the privilege of choosing

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:53.720
whether to be white to a certain extent,

00:37:53.720 --> 00:37:55.590
you know, and whether to do that,

00:37:55.590 --> 00:37:56.830
which is an interesting...

00:37:56.830 --> 00:37:59.810
So here's another question
from the audience.

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:04.550
What about the role of Black Jews?

00:38:04.550 --> 00:38:06.970
And this is something,
maybe a question, Anthony,

00:38:06.970 --> 00:38:09.277
so, you know, we have,
of course, you know,

00:38:09.277 --> 00:38:12.160
the kind of celebrity examples
like Sammy Davis, Jr.,

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:15.110
but also, you know, there,
maybe more problematically,

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:18.160
the Black Israelite movement
and things like that.

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:19.960
How should we think about those groups?

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:21.760
A question, really, for both of you.

00:38:23.341 --> 00:38:25.630
- I think as far as Black
Israelites are concerned,

00:38:25.630 --> 00:38:30.630
it's complicated because
in that case, it really is,

00:38:30.850 --> 00:38:33.220
I think quite directly, an appropriation

00:38:36.253 --> 00:38:40.810
of Jewish religiosity, as a
form of religious expression.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:47.920
And I think there's a
lot of different aspects

00:38:47.920 --> 00:38:52.920
of Black diasporic history that
go into that sort of impulse

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:59.363
to appropriate very directly,

00:39:00.820 --> 00:39:03.973
this way of expressing
one's self religiously.

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:07.890
It's very hard for me
to actually figure out

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:11.290
how that fits in, and it's
something about which I'm afraid,

00:39:11.290 --> 00:39:13.610
I'm a little ignorant.

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:16.093
It's something that I've been
meaning to learn more about,

00:39:16.093 --> 00:39:19.250
just because it's interesting in general,

00:39:19.250 --> 00:39:21.640
but I don't know.

00:39:21.640 --> 00:39:24.490
In a way it seems like...

00:39:27.772 --> 00:39:31.470
A sort of fulfillment, an
embodiment of this idea

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:33.530
of the paradigm, but it goes off into such

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:36.970
a very definitive direction that I think,

00:39:36.970 --> 00:39:41.970
in many ways, if they're not
looked on with hostility,

00:39:43.350 --> 00:39:45.780
they're looked on with a
great amount of curiosity

00:39:45.780 --> 00:39:50.780
as a bizarity in relationship
to the normative aspects

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:53.253
of American Judaism.

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:56.043
- Right.

00:39:56.043 --> 00:39:57.600
And I'll just throw in
that obviously mainstream,

00:39:57.600 --> 00:39:59.180
white Jews are not particularly interested

00:39:59.180 --> 00:40:02.190
in Black Jews of any kind.

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:05.240
There's not like a huge
community outreach effort

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:07.290
to bring, to open up conversations

00:40:07.290 --> 00:40:08.123
with the more fringe groups

00:40:08.123 --> 00:40:11.030
or to bring in, and
that's part, obviously,

00:40:11.030 --> 00:40:15.280
of Jewish, Jews not being
the converting people,

00:40:15.280 --> 00:40:17.350
but you know, that's there too.

00:40:17.350 --> 00:40:19.960
I mean, there there's, I just
wanna just quickly go back

00:40:19.960 --> 00:40:21.640
to the question about Jews in the South,

00:40:21.640 --> 00:40:24.310
since I have particular interest
there, in an earlier era

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:26.750
then than what Anthony was talking about,

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:28.435
which is that most of
the Jews in the South

00:40:28.435 --> 00:40:32.330
in the pre-World War
II era are German Jews.

00:40:32.330 --> 00:40:35.740
And they're, to borrow a word
that they wouldn't have used,

00:40:35.740 --> 00:40:39.780
'cause it's a more Eastern
European, Yiddish kind of word,

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:40.960
they were alrightniks.

00:40:40.960 --> 00:40:44.900
I mean, they were really
satisfied with their position

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:47.840
by and large, as quasi-white people,

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:49.670
or apprentice white people.

00:40:49.670 --> 00:40:52.330
And that's a really, that made
for really complicated math

00:40:52.330 --> 00:40:55.900
in moments of crisis when, you know,

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:56.810
and obviously I wrote a book about it,

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:57.643
the Leo Frank case.

00:40:57.643 --> 00:40:59.190
So that's a moment of real crisis,

00:40:59.190 --> 00:41:02.656
where Jews make great
efforts to try to demonstrate

00:41:02.656 --> 00:41:05.130
to the white people around them,

00:41:05.130 --> 00:41:08.060
that they are really
better than Black people,

00:41:08.060 --> 00:41:10.940
and really more trustworthy
and safer and all that.

00:41:10.940 --> 00:41:12.070
Yeah.

00:41:12.070 --> 00:41:14.670
- Of course, there's also,
the Israeli dimension

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:15.620
