And later on, when Jews
sort of stepped out

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:44.420
of the central role to be, you know,

00:52:44.420 --> 00:52:46.970
the record label owners and
the producers and so on.

00:52:46.970 --> 00:52:49.170
So there's like a history
here that I think's impossible

00:52:49.170 --> 00:52:52.429
to ignore, when we think
about The Notorious RGB.

00:52:52.429 --> 00:52:56.560
The conversation I've been
in, and it's been fascinating,

00:52:56.560 --> 00:52:58.730
and particularly interesting to see

00:52:58.730 --> 00:53:01.210
all of the African-American
people who have jumped in

00:53:01.210 --> 00:53:04.623
to the conversation to
say, Oh, yeah, that's,

00:53:05.920 --> 00:53:07.860
everyone knows that's
been bad for a long time.

00:53:07.860 --> 00:53:09.200
And I wasn't aware, like I didn't,

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:11.190
I feel benighted 'cause I wasn't aware

00:53:11.190 --> 00:53:13.840
that there was a whole
conversation already about it.

00:53:14.970 --> 00:53:15.803
- Okay.

00:53:16.780 --> 00:53:20.633
So, you know, a couple of
people have noted that,

00:53:21.920 --> 00:53:24.560
or have wondered whether this,

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:26.490
the Black-Jewish relations paradigm,

00:53:26.490 --> 00:53:28.790
is it only really something in the past?

00:53:28.790 --> 00:53:33.790
Or can we talk about moments
or points of convergence today?

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:37.310
You know, one of them being, for instance,

00:53:37.310 --> 00:53:42.083
that that Blacks and Jews are
two of the most loyal groups

00:53:43.860 --> 00:53:45.120
within the Democratic party.

00:53:45.120 --> 00:53:48.250
In fact, their voting
records are pretty similar,

00:53:48.250 --> 00:53:50.258
for the most part.

00:53:50.258 --> 00:53:53.790
And, you know, to try, we only
have a couple minutes left.

00:53:53.790 --> 00:53:56.400
So I think this is gonna
be the last question,

00:53:56.400 --> 00:54:00.670
but is there...

00:54:01.670 --> 00:54:03.930
Are there moments of, of kind of,

00:54:03.930 --> 00:54:06.950
are there positive things that
we might look towards today,

00:54:06.950 --> 00:54:09.740
politically useful ways of thinking

00:54:09.740 --> 00:54:14.023
about the Black-Jewish relationship today?

00:54:15.660 --> 00:54:16.493
- Ever so briefly-

00:54:16.493 --> 00:54:19.500
- Without whitewashing what's
wrong about it, but yeah.

00:54:19.500 --> 00:54:20.333
- Of course.

00:54:21.460 --> 00:54:23.270
Ever so briefly, I'd
like to address the fact

00:54:23.270 --> 00:54:26.330
that the reason why we
even have the ability

00:54:26.330 --> 00:54:31.320
to name check the people
who did take the effort

00:54:31.320 --> 00:54:36.320
to align themselves with nascent movements

00:54:36.490 --> 00:54:41.350
of racial equality is
because they showed up

00:54:41.350 --> 00:54:42.958
in physical space.

00:54:42.958 --> 00:54:46.490
They did not show up in rhetorical space.

00:54:46.490 --> 00:54:49.640
They did not release a concept record

00:54:49.640 --> 00:54:52.110
about what they thought their relationship

00:54:52.110 --> 00:54:52.943
was to Black people.

00:54:52.943 --> 00:54:55.639
They did not write an essay,
even though as an essayist,

00:54:55.639 --> 00:54:58.440
I think it still is
important work to do that,

00:54:58.440 --> 00:55:00.730
to get those ideas out into the world.

00:55:00.730 --> 00:55:04.753
They showed up in physical American space,

00:55:05.690 --> 00:55:07.723
an American space in which,

00:55:08.740 --> 00:55:11.950
where they were supposed
to be in order to retain

00:55:11.950 --> 00:55:16.170
the status quo was very
distinctly written out.

00:55:16.170 --> 00:55:17.960
They crossed those boundaries,

00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:20.600
they crossed the imaginary boundaries

00:55:20.600 --> 00:55:25.600
of what the American Jewish-Black relation

00:55:25.950 --> 00:55:29.100
was supposed to be, and they
entered into physical space

00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:31.670
and they showed up on
behalf of Black people.

00:55:31.670 --> 00:55:36.290
And I think as long as
you are trying to do that

00:55:36.290 --> 00:55:39.680
to the best of your ability,
you will find yourself

00:55:43.380 --> 00:55:46.290
answering this question, directly,

00:55:46.290 --> 00:55:49.013
in time, and once again,
in American space.

00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:52.473
- Great.

00:55:52.473 --> 00:55:55.325
I mean, I have almost
nothing to add to that

00:55:55.325 --> 00:55:58.603
other than, you know, if
you can join your union.

00:56:00.370 --> 00:56:01.620
I'm just trying to think of literal,

00:56:01.620 --> 00:56:03.537
street-level places
where African-Americans

00:56:03.537 --> 00:56:05.439
and Jews might still come in contact

00:56:05.439 --> 00:56:07.730
and the growing union
movement, and particularly,

00:56:07.730 --> 00:56:10.723
teachers' unions, particularly
trying to bring together,

00:56:12.310 --> 00:56:13.870
sort of main classroom teachers

00:56:13.870 --> 00:56:15.660
with paraprofessional teachers,

00:56:15.660 --> 00:56:17.532
there's gonna be some
really interesting ways

00:56:17.532 --> 00:56:19.860
that I think Jews, in particular,

00:56:19.860 --> 00:56:22.950
will be challenged to
imagine as I think Anthony

00:56:22.950 --> 00:56:25.107
was just really hinting at,

00:56:25.107 --> 00:56:27.970
imagining solidarity with Black people

00:56:27.970 --> 00:56:30.850
that will mean Jews showing up for things

00:56:30.850 --> 00:56:33.280
that might not immediately benefit them,

00:56:33.280 --> 00:56:35.690
but that might be kind of just larger,

00:56:35.690 --> 00:56:38.970
social justice achievements
that Jews care about,

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:40.520
or should care about.
- Right.

00:56:40.520 --> 00:56:41.720
The Civil Rights Movement originally

00:56:41.720 --> 00:56:44.450
was not an overdetermined phenomenon.

00:56:44.450 --> 00:56:46.490
People did not know how
it was going to end.

00:56:46.490 --> 00:56:48.880
- Right.
- Which makes the actions

00:56:48.880 --> 00:56:50.391
of Jews in that particular period,

00:56:50.391 --> 00:56:54.800
who were a part of that
movement, even more valorous

00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:57.240
in my mind, because this was once again,

00:56:57.240 --> 00:56:58.803
like a nascent movement.

00:56:59.729 --> 00:57:04.680
There were not terms which
were dictated to the people

00:57:04.680 --> 00:57:09.680
who were in this movement
in order to assure

00:57:10.070 --> 00:57:12.970
that they were going
to join this movement.

00:57:12.970 --> 00:57:14.810
We really do have these models,

00:57:14.810 --> 00:57:17.890
and if we follow these models in space,

00:57:17.890 --> 00:57:20.780
if we show up, like I said, once again,

00:57:20.780 --> 00:57:22.543
we have answers to these questions.

00:57:24.160 --> 00:57:26.810
- Okay, well I think we
have to leave it there.

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:28.350
It's 6:00.

00:57:28.350 --> 00:57:31.440
Clearly, this conversation
could go on a lot longer

00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:33.080
and fortunately it will.

00:57:33.080 --> 00:57:35.500
And I encourage everyone to tune in

00:57:35.500 --> 00:57:38.500
for our whole lecture series
this semester, which again,

00:57:38.500 --> 00:57:43.180
you can find on the YPSA, YPSA website.

00:57:43.180 --> 00:57:47.870
And my apologies to the
people who asked questions

00:57:47.870 --> 00:57:50.670
that I didn't get to ask.

00:57:50.670 --> 00:57:52.310
They were really fascinating questions.

00:57:52.310 --> 00:57:55.080
And I thank the audience,
but let's all give

00:57:55.080 --> 00:57:58.302
our special thanks to Jeff and Anthony.

00:57:58.302 --> 00:58:01.350
One of the downsides of
the webinar format is

00:58:01.350 --> 00:58:04.977
that they can't hear you, but
I will clap for everybody.

00:58:04.977 --> 00:58:09.370
(clapping) So thank you very,
very much for this really,

00:58:09.370 --> 00:58:11.900
really stimulating conversation, so.

00:58:11.900 --> 00:58:13.825
- Thank you so much for having us.

00:58:13.825 --> 00:58:14.658
- Thank you, really.

00:58:14.658 --> 00:58:15.890
Thank you everybody for showing up.

00:58:15.890 --> 00:58:17.150
Great to see your names, att least.

00:58:17.150 --> 00:58:18.959
Really appreciate it.

00:58:18.959 --> 00:58:19.800
- [Mauri] Okay.

00:58:19.800 --> 00:58:21.113
- Thanks. Take care.

